 we are speaking with Professor  Rachel Sherman about her new book uneasy  Street the anxieties of affluence we'll  pause here and continue our conversation  with her we're continuing our  conversation with Rachel Sherman who is  associate professor of sociology at the  new school in New York also author of  the book uneasy Street the anxieties of  affluence rachel at the end of the first  part we were talking about sort of the  way in which the wealthy individuals you  interviewed seemed sort of adapted to  their standard of living as a new  baseline did you sort of experience that  yourself because there's a point in the  book where you write about sort of you  were adapting and sympathizing with and  having compassion for the difficulties  of managing the renovations of three  properties that someone might have  including one in the Hamptons or  whatever did you start to sort of see  the world through the types of problems  that really rich people have as well  yeah I mean I think that example comes  up particularly because the women that I  had spoken to when I started feeling  like that had relatively significant  problems with their husbands around the  control of money and I just felt very  sympathetic to the lack of recognition  that they were getting from their  husbands and then I felt like it was  distracting me from the fact that the  problems that they were talking about  how to do with the renovation of the  home in the Hamptons or whatever but the  broader issue I mean in general I I  found it very difficult to retain some  level of objectivity right we're so used  to judging wealthy people and just  thinking that there are expenditures you  know are ridiculous or are reasonable or  you know that they're nice people or bad  people or whatever it's hard to kind of  get out of that but I think as a I had  the advantage of being a relatively  experienced you know sociological  interviewer so I already know a little  bit how to how to minimize that but  being caught between the kind of common  judgments that that you know circulate  about rich people into which I'm  certainly  immune and the empathy or the  identification that interviewers often  feel with the people that they're  talking to is a little bit of a fine  line to walk yeah well in the interviews  you do ask questions that sort of try to  get at and really you have a whole  chapter about this how these wealthy  people you spoke to I don't know if it  defend is the right way but I'm gonna  use that word and then you can you can  sort of correct me or point me in a  better direction defend the wealth that  they have from a sort of deserving it  standpoint and you would see a lot of  different lines of reasoning that that  in the people you interviewed right  listen we pay lots of taxes so we're  doing what we need to do we pay the  taxes on them on the money we make and  thus it's ok or listen we still work in  other ways we take care of the kids and  do a lot of volunteering or work  wherever it is that has some kind of  social good etc talk to me a little bit  about the defenses of wealth yeah I'm  not sure I mean defenses is a reasonable  word I think justifications might be a  better word although justification kind  of has it has a connotation of  insincerity which I don't think that  these people are insincere in talking  about this I think what they're doing is  highlighting the content of the  judgments that are very broadly made of  wealthy people in American culture and  possibly elsewhere of you know you  deserve your wealth if you've worked  hard for it or you know a little bit if  you still work hard or if you you know  so if you are as you said like a  stay-at-home mother you're trying to  frame yourself as working even though  you're not working for pay or if you're  inheritor you're trying to frame  yourself as working in a job even though  you know that you didn't actually earn  the money that you're living on but the  idea of being a hard worker whether or  not that explains your money and the  idea of consuming not being an  ostentatious materialistic you know  greedy consumer and then the idea of  giving money back you know some people  refer to their taxes as you said many  people refer to their charitable  contributions or to volunteerism a lot  of people also talked about a kind of  obligation to be aware of their  privilege so even though  they sort of don't want to talk about it  they also feel like they need privately  to recognize it and they want to raise  kids who are also sort of morally worthy  good people and who themselves have all  of these traits so in those ways they  kind of draw these distinctions between  people who are entitled who are morally  worthy of welcome people who are not so  other than I mean you talked about the  more progressive people that you spoke  to tended to have more liberal guilt  about their wealth but sort of general  putting aside the broader political  identification were there specific  factors you found that made some wealthy  people more attuned to issues of wealth  inequality and the problems of the less  wealthy and the poor than others whether  it's you know they inherited money  versus they they earned it through work  or investments or whatever else what  other factors might we point to well I  think people I mean this is again some  of the opposite of the inheritor piece  but people who are actually upwardly  mobile are more you know they have  family members and friends from their  childhoods or you know communities of  origin who have much less than they do  so those people also tend to you know  keep others in their mind and the people  who have really a lot of well they're  not upwardly mobile in the traditional  sense and then many of them started  middle class or upper middle class to  begin with but then if you have 50  million dollars you're you are upwardly  mobile relative to what you started with  so they have a consciousness about that  too and I think a lot of these people  have they're not unaware that like four  people exist right they I think they all  know that one of the things I think that  happens in stereotypes of rich people is  that we imagined that rich people are  kind of incredibly stupid and shallow I  mean I mean you know these are very  well-educated people who live in the  world so they they they know that people  who have less than they do exist it's  just a partly a question of how they  manage that knowledge and some of them  take a much more aníbal approach which  says you know we're never gonna change  the system it's not my job to change the  system I'm entitled to this because I  work hard and I'm a nice person and I  you know I'm a reasonable parent so I'm  gonna give some money away and you know  I'm not going to be that conflicted  about it those people still feel the  need that I  is talking about to be morally worthy  right they still don't want to show off  their money they don't want to hoard  their money they don't want their kids  to be obnoxious or entitled right the  concept of entitlement is something that  across the board my respondents really  didn't like but the way that they sort  of see those obligations does bearing  it's interesting that you talk about you  know these are not stupid people or  ignorant people they're well-educated  I'm curious to hear your thoughts about  you know there's been a lot of different  studies about how wealth can sort of  change one's perception of reality  there's studies about the reduction of  compassion on average of wealthier  people there's this UC Berkeley study  which showed that there will be sort of  a rationalization of advantage by by  convincing oneself that they deserve it  and you explore that in the book with  this idea of hey I worked hard  etc studies that show even even sort of  mundane things like richer people are  more likely to ignore pedestrians or  there's a UCSF study about the wealthier  you are the worse you are on average at  reading the facial expressions of others  this type of thing what's your thought  after doing all the research and  interviews for your book about all of  that research that's out there yeah and  a lot of that research relies on social  psychological methods sort of  experimental methods that take place in  laboratories and I think those kinds of  methods you know sometimes just get at  something different than what I'm  looking at and I think that we have so  little research on rich people it may be  one explanation would be like there's  different kinds of rich people and some  of them you know have all of the traits  the sort of negative traits that have  been identified in these primarily  social psychological studies and some of  them have you know a stronger moral  compass of the kind that I'm identifying  or whatever but I also just think that  these are different aspects of having  the experience of having privileged so  you know talking explicitly with me  about some of these issues is gonna be  different from how people are going to  be in a lab although it doesn't matter  which people are in the lab which is  another part of this and I think what's  important is to just keep exploring  these different  and these kind of characteristics  because I don't think that they're  necessarily mutually exclusive right and  the point that I'm trying to emphasize  is not so much like rich people are  moral right but you know they're not as  evil as we think that they are my point  is more about thinking well why are we  invested in judging them as moral or not  moral no and what are the criteria that  we're using to do that so do we really  think that I'm society with the levels  of inequality that we are currently  seeing that it is okay to be you know to  have ten million dollars or 50 million  dollars or 50 billion dollars just you  know on the basis of being a nice person  or having worked hard or you know not  living in a McMansion right which and  these are the criteria that my  respondents are laying out and they are  also criteria that I think resonate with  people who who aren't rich or certainly  aren't as rich as they are do you know  how many of the people you interviewed  have read the book and is anybody upset  about how they were portrayed I don't  know and I don't know I do care and I  would be interested to know I mean again  you know I have really tried to to not  do what I think a lot of people might do  in this kind of research which is be  really voyeuristic or you know kind of  talked a lot about their homes or the  spending that they do have and and make  nice you know make the judgment myself  that they are or are not reasonable  consumers right that for example that's  a totally relative to category and I  just want to take myself out of making  those judgments I think that's not them  appreciate that I'm trying to understand  and represent their perspective but on  the other hand I have ended up talking  about things that I think are hard for  many of them to talk about and yeah you  know that might be something that they  don't like I have gone very far out of  my way to preserve their anonymity so I  hope that that makes a difference too  we've been speaking about the book  uneasy Street the anxieties of affluence  we've been speaking with the book's  author professor Rachel Sherman thanks  so much for talking to me about it I  found the book super interesting  great thank you thanks for having me
 

